Original commit body from 3c6e2b8e5e63e12eb455b5758cff2f5951f9d63d:

Mark Read / Mark Unread rebuilt the item table through
_reload_active_table() + _restore_selection(). _populate_table clears the
table and leaves the cursor on row 0, which scrolls the viewport to the
top, and only _reload_after_write captured and restored the offset. So
with the list scrolled at all, every Mark Read click jumped it to the top.

The same unguarded pair sat behind the status, tag and meta:tagged
toggles, clear/restore filters, the full-ID toggle, reorder (</>), the
edit-mode toggle and the tier change on resize. All of them, and
_reload_after_write, now go through one _rebuild_in_place():

- capture the scroll offset and the cursor row, rebuild, put the cursor
  back on the selected item, restore the offset after the next refresh;
- if the selected item is gone (Mark Read on an unread row that a filter
  hides), keep the cursor at the same row index, clamped;
- if the cursor was on screen before, keep it on screen: the restored
  offset is applied immediately and the cursor scrolled into view, which
  moves only when rows above it were filtered away. A cursor the user
  wheel-scrolled out of view is left alone, so a background reload
  never yanks the view back to it.

The live-search path (typing in the filter input) and Esc-dismiss still
start from the top on purpose.

Tests: TestRebuildKeepsViewport (Mark Read button at wide size; seven
rebuild paths; reorder; cursor brought into view when the rows above it
vanish; cursor index kept when its row vanishes) plus pre-mount guards.
Tracker package coverage 100% in isolation.

